{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1125434-golden-age-of-science-fiction-de2","id":1125434,"title":"Golden Age of Science Fiction [DE2]","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-08-05T18:20:06-07:00","id":1125434,"title":"Golden Age of Science Fiction [DE2]","content":"So lots of speculation and excitement about this era. I'm favouring Deviant, Mummy, Promethean, and Demon, but there could be lots of neat things that could be done with other lines.<br /> <br /> Key themes off the top of my head:<ul><li><b>The Oncoming Future</b>: While everyone loves robots and space colonies and all of that, it is worth pointing out that science fiction is also speculative fiction. A huge number of modern conveniences that we take for granted started in short stories. How does that impact creatures like Vampires, Beasts, and Mummies that can take the long view on history?</li> <li><b>Utopia vs Dystopia</b>: Science Fiction imagines worlds that might be, revolutions increasing the standard of living for all, and forever changing the human race. At the same time, this is the era of Stalin and Hitler, and one can only imagine what would happen if these megalomanics got their hands on such power.</li> <li><b>Humanity's Frailty</b>: Hiroshima and Nagasaki at the end of the era confirmed the darkest fears of scientists: we could destroy ourselves, completely and utterly in an instant. That is a sobering thought, and dystopian literature explored just how much damage we could do to ourselves.</li> </ul>If you look at Isaac Asimov specifically, all but one of the works collected in I, Robot are published in this period and the Foundation Series is well underway.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-size:14px\"><a href=\"https://tinyurl.com/2p8ss75f\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Chris H</b></a>/<b>EndDaysEngine</b> | <a 